## Solution

**Setting up the permutation.** Label the bays 1–26 and the drones by their starting bay (A=1, B=2, …, Z=26). The rerouting protocol is a permutation σ on bay positions, where "the drone in bay *i* moves to bay σ(*i*)". Under this convention, applying σ to a configuration *C* yields *C*′ with *C*′[σ(*i*)] = *C*[*i*], i.e. *C*′[*j*] = *C*[σ⁻¹(*j*)].

**Extracting σ from the given data.** Since the same σ carries T₁→T₂, we have T₂[*j*] = T₁[σ⁻¹(*j*)], so σ⁻¹(*j*) is the position of drone T₂[*j*] within T₁. Computing for all 26 positions:

| *j* | T₂[*j*] | position in T₁ | σ⁻¹(*j*) | σ(*i*) |
|-----|---------|-----------------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Z | 10 | 10 | σ(10)=1 |
| 2 | G | 17 | 17 | σ(17)=2 |
| 3 | Y | 15 | 15 | σ(15)=3 |
| 4 | K | 23 | 23 | σ(23)=4 |
| 5 | T | 9 | 9 | σ(9)=5 |
| 6 | E | 16 | 16 | σ(16)=6 |
| 7 | J | 1 | 1 | σ(1)=7 |
| 8 | M | 14 | 14 | σ(14)=8 |
| 9 | U | 20 | 20 | σ(20)=9 |
| 10 | X | 26 | 26 | σ(26)=10 |
| 11 | S | 18 | 18 | σ(18)=11 |
| 12 | O | 3 | 3 | σ(3)=12 |
| 13 | D | 22 | 22 | σ(22)=13 |
| 14 | V | 13 | 13 | σ(13)=14 |
| 15 | L | 25 | 25 | σ(25)=15 |
| 16 | I | 5 | 5 | σ(5)=16 |
| 17 | A | 7 | 7 | σ(7)=17 |
| 18 | H | 19 | 19 | σ(19)=18 |
| 19 | N | 8 | 8 | σ(8)=19 |
| 20 | F | 21 | 21 | σ(21)=20 |
| 21 | P | 6 | 6 | σ(6)=21 |
| 22 | W | 4 | 4 | σ(4)=22 |
| 23 | R | 11 | 11 | σ(11)=23 |
| 24 | Q | 2 | 2 | σ(2)=24 |
| 25 | C | 12 | 12 | σ(12)=25 |
| 26 | B | 24 | 24 | σ(24)=26 |

**Verification against T₀→T₁.** Checking T₁[*j*] = T₀[σ⁻¹(*j*)] for every *j*: e.g. T₁[1]=T₀[10]=J ✓, T₁[4]=T₀[23]=W ✓, T₁[10]=T₀[26]=Z ✓, …, T₁[26]=T₀[24]=X ✓. All 26 positions match, confirming σ is the unique protocol consistent with both transitions.

**Finding the cycle structure of σ:**

- **Cycle 1:** 1 → 7 → 17 → 2 → 24 → 26 → 10 → **1**. Length **7**.
- **Cycle 2:** 3 → 12 → 25 → 15 → **3**. Length **4**.
- **Cycle 3:** 4 → 22 → 13 → 14 → 8 → 19 → 18 → 11 → 23 → **4**. Length **9**.
- **Cycle 4:** 5 → 16 → 6 → 21 → 20 → 9 → **5**. Length **6**.

Coverage check: 7 + 4 + 9 + 6 = 26 ✓ (every bay appears exactly once).

**Computing the order.** The protocol returns all drones to their original bays exactly when σⁿ = identity, which first happens at

$$n = \operatorname{lcm}(7,\, 4,\, 9,\, 6).$$

Prime factorizations: 7 = 7, 4 = 2², 9 = 3², 6 = 2·3. Taking the maximum power of each prime:

$$\operatorname{lcm} = 2^{2}\cdot 3^{2}\cdot 7 = 4\cdot 9\cdot 7 = 252.$$

**Verification of divisibility:** 252/7 = 36 ✓, 252/4 = 63 ✓, 252/9 = 28 ✓, 252/6 = 42 ✓. No smaller positive integer is divisible by all four cycle lengths (since 4 and 9 together already require 36, and including 7 forces 252; 6 adds no new prime power).

**Answer:** The smallest positive integer *n* such that Tₙ = T₀ is

$$\boxed{252}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
